    "body": "A related concern from the same space — about _when_ module-script completion is observable, not just _whether_. I thought it would be better to append to this thread versus try and open a new one. And, this issue felt like a better fit than #127.\n\nI maintain a small, in-browser test-runner library, and currently I have no way to truly _await_ the test registration phase because there’s no reliable signal for when a module script has finished loading (e.g., `<script type=\"module\" src=\"has-top-level-await.js\"></script>`). The closest event is `DOMContentLoaded`, but that breaks the moment there’s a top-level await in the module. To work around it, I have to expose something like a `waitFor` function that users manually invoke to keep the registration window open.\n\nCompare this with Node’s `node:test`, which knows _exactly_ when a module is done loading and can do the same thing seamlessly.\n\nI could make stronger assumptions if modules were always loaded via dynamic `import(…)`, but that’s not an option for my use case — I need something more from the browser platform.\n\nWould love to see this move forward. Top-level await (no matter how anyone feels about it) is idiomatic, and projects like mine would expect it to _just work_.",
